import json
from pathlib import Path
cards=[]
def c(group,title,eq,meaning,steps,pitfall,prompt):
 cards.append(dict(id=str(len(cards)+1),group=group,title=title,equations=eq,meaning=meaning,steps=steps,pitfall=pitfall,prompt=prompt))
c('Foundations','Notation & SI units',['E: V/m; B: tesla','D: C/m²; H: A/m','c = 1/√(ε₀μ₀)'], 'Bold quantities in textbooks are vectors. Here E, B, D, H, J, A, r, p and m denote vectors when used in vector equations. ∇· is divergence; ∇× is curl. Partial derivatives hold position fixed.', 'Use SI consistently. ρ is volume charge density, σ is surface charge density, λ is line charge density. ε and μ are absolute material parameters.', 'Do not mix Gaussian and SI formulas. The symbol σ also denotes conductivity when explicitly stated.', 'What are the units of electric flux?')
c('Foundations','Vector calculus',['∮ E·da = ∫ (∇·E) dτ','∮ E·dl = ∫ (∇×E)·da','∇×(∇×E) = ∇(∇·E) − ∇²E'], 'Divergence measures local outward flux. Curl measures local circulation. Gauss and Stokes connect differential and integral field laws.', 'Choose a closed surface for divergence; an oriented open surface with boundary for curl. The surface normal and boundary direction obey the right-hand rule.', 'A vanishing divergence does not imply a vanishing field. A curl-free field is globally a gradient only with suitable topology.', 'Why does ∇·B = 0 not imply B = 0?')
c('Foundations','Lorentz force',['F = q(E + v×B)','P = F·v = q E·v','r = mv⊥/(|q|B)'], 'Electric fields can change kinetic energy. Magnetic forces bend motion but do no work on a point charge.', 'For a uniform magnetic field, split velocity into parallel and perpendicular parts. The parallel part stays constant; the perpendicular part circles.', 'The radius formula is nonrelativistic. At relativistic speed replace mv⊥ by p⊥.', 'Explain helical motion in a uniform magnetic field.')
c('Maxwell','Maxwell: microscopic',['∇·E = ρ/ε₀','∇·B = 0','∇×E = −∂B/∂t','∇×B = μ₀J + μ₀ε₀ ∂E/∂t'], 'Charge sources electric flux. Magnetic flux has no sources. Changing B drives circulating E. Current and changing E drive circulating B.', 'These equations use total charge and current, including bound sources. Pair them with the Lorentz force and material/source dynamics.', 'Maxwell equations alone do not specify matter response or boundary and initial data.', 'State the physical meaning of each Maxwell equation.')
c('Maxwell','Maxwell: integral',['∮ E·da = Qenc/ε₀','∮ B·da = 0','∮ E·dl = −dΦB/dt','∮ B·dl = μ₀Ienc + μ₀ε₀ dΦE/dt'], 'Integral laws connect fields to enclosed sources and changing flux.', 'Use a fixed loop and fixed spanning surface for these induction formulas. Choose symmetry before pulling a field outside an integral.', 'For a moving circuit the emf is ∮(E + vwire×B)·dl; the electric circulation alone is insufficient.', 'Why can Ampère’s loop use either side of a charging capacitor?')
c('Maxwell','Charge conservation',['∂ρ/∂t + ∇·J = 0','dQ/dt = −∮ J·da'], 'Charge leaving a volume reduces its charge. This local conservation law follows from Ampère–Maxwell and Gauss.', 'Take divergence of Ampère–Maxwell. Use divergence of a curl = 0, then substitute ∇·E = ρ/ε₀.', 'Dropping displacement current in a time-dependent problem can violate charge conservation.', 'Derive the continuity equation from Maxwell’s equations.')
c('Maxwell','Fields in matter',['D = ε₀E + P','H = B/μ₀ − M','∇·D = ρfree','∇×H = Jfree + ∂D/∂t'], 'Polarization P describes electric dipole density. Magnetization M describes magnetic dipole density. D and H separate free sources from bound sources.', 'For linear isotropic media, D = εE and B = μH. Use constitutive relations only when their assumptions hold.', 'D is not always parallel to E; ε may be a tensor, frequency dependent or nonlinear.', 'Why does Gauss’s law for D use free charge only?')
c('Maxwell','Bound sources',['ρb = −∇·P; σb = P·n̂','Jb = ∇×M + ∂P/∂t','Kb = M×n̂'], 'Spatial variation of polarization creates bound charge. Magnetization and time-changing polarization create bound currents.', 'n̂ points outward from the material. Include both volume and surface terms when replacing material response by equivalent sources.', 'Uniform P can have zero volume charge and nonzero surface charge.', 'Find the bound charges on a uniformly polarized sphere.')
c('Electrostatics','Coulomb & potential',['E(r) = (1/4πε₀) ∫ ρ(r′) R̂/R² dτ′','V(r) = (1/4πε₀) ∫ ρ(r′)/R dτ′','E = −∇V; R = |r−r′|'], 'In electrostatics the electric field is conservative and potential differences give work per unit charge.', 'Choose the source element dq, write source-to-observation displacement, exploit symmetry, integrate, and check far-field behavior.', 'For an infinite line or plane, V cannot generally be set to zero at infinity. Choose a finite reference.', 'Why is potential often easier to integrate than the field?')
c('Electrostatics','Gauss: common symmetries',['Sphere outside: E = Q/(4πε₀r²)','Infinite line: E = λ/(2πε₀r)','Infinite sheet: |E| = |σ|/(2ε₀)'], 'These magnitudes follow only when symmetry fixes direction and makes the field constant on the useful parts of a Gaussian surface.', 'Choose sphere, coaxial cylinder or pillbox. Compute enclosed charge at the observation radius, then solve the flux equation.', 'Gauss’s law always holds, but an arbitrary surface does not make E constant.', 'Find E inside a uniformly charged solid sphere.')
c('Electrostatics','Poisson & Laplace',['∇²V = −ρ/ε₀','ρ = 0 ⇒ ∇²V = 0'], 'Potential obeys Poisson’s equation in vacuum and Laplace’s equation in charge-free regions. Boundary data selects the solution.', 'Identify each region; solve its PDE; match boundary conditions; apply behavior at infinity. Dirichlet data fixes V uniquely. Neumann data fixes it up to a constant with compatibility.', 'ρ = 0 does not imply V = 0. Distant charges can produce a field in the region.', 'Explain why boundary values can determine an entire electrostatic field.')
c('Electrostatics','Separation in a sphere',['V(r,θ) = Σₗ [Aₗrˡ + Bₗ/rˡ⁺¹] Pₗ(cosθ)'], 'This is the axisymmetric solution of Laplace’s equation in spherical coordinates, where l = 0,1,2,… .', 'Discard B terms in a region containing r = 0 if no singularity exists. Discard growing A terms at infinity when the physical boundary condition requires decay. Expand boundary data in Legendre polynomials.', 'An imposed uniform field requires a growing l = 1 potential term at infinity; do not discard it blindly.', 'Which term represents a uniform electric field along z?')
c('Electrostatics','Method of images',['Image of q at z=a: −q at z=−a','Fz = −q²/(16πε₀a²)'], 'A grounded infinite conducting plane at z = 0 can be replaced mathematically by an image charge when solving the physical region z > 0.', 'Construct V using real and image charges. Check V = 0 on the plane and the far boundary. Invoke uniqueness, then evaluate the image field at q.', 'Images are not physical charges. Energy is half the real charge times its image potential: U = −q²/(16πε₀a).', 'Why is the image method valid only in the selected physical region?')
c('Electrostatics','Conductors & capacitance',['Electrostatic conductor: Einside = 0','Eoutside·n̂ = σ/ε₀ (vacuum)','C = Q/ΔV','U = Q²/(2C) = ½C(ΔV)²'], 'An equilibrium conductor is equipotential. Excess charge resides on its surface. Capacitance relates opposite charges on a two-conductor capacitor to potential difference.', 'Find E for the geometry, integrate ΔV, then divide Q by ΔV. Parallel plates with negligible edges: C = εA/d.', 'A conductor in a time-varying field need not have E = 0. A battery can exchange energy during mechanical motion.', 'Why do isolated and battery-connected capacitor problems have different energy changes?')
c('Electrostatics','Electric dipoles & multipoles',['p = ∫ r′ρ(r′) dτ′','V ≈ (1/4πε₀)[Q/r + (p·r̂)/r²]','E dip = [3(p·r̂)r̂ − p]/(4πε₀r³)','τ = p×E; U = −p·E'], 'Far from a localized distribution, monopole, dipole and higher terms organize the field in powers of source size divided by distance.', 'Compute total Q first. If Q = 0, compute p. Use the first nonzero term for the leading far field.', 'The dipole approximation requires distance much larger than source size. p depends on origin if Q ≠ 0.', 'Why does a neutral localized source often have an r⁻³ electric field?')
c('Interfaces','Boundary conditions',['n̂·(D₂−D₁) = σfree','n̂·(B₂−B₁) = 0','n̂×(E₂−E₁) = 0','n̂×(H₂−H₁) = Kfree'], 'At a stationary ordinary interface, normal D jumps with free surface charge and tangential H jumps with free surface current. Normal B and tangential E are continuous.', 'Take n̂ from region 1 into region 2. Derive normal relations with a thin pillbox and tangential relations with a shrinking rectangular loop.', 'These forms assume nonsingular time-varying fluxes at the interface. Tangential E continuity does not mean normal E is continuous.', 'Derive the normal D condition using a pillbox.')
c('Magnetostatics','Biot–Savart & Ampère',['B = (μ₀/4π) ∫ J(r′)×R̂/R² dτ′','∮ B·dl = μ₀Ienc','Wire: B = μ₀I/(2πr)','Long solenoid: B ≈ μ₀nI'], 'Steady currents produce magnetic fields. Ampère’s law becomes an easy algebraic solution only with sufficient symmetry.', 'Use Biot–Savart for a general loop. For an infinite wire choose a circular Amperian loop; for a long solenoid use a rectangle.', 'Steady current requires ∇·J = 0. A finite isolated current segment cannot sustain steady current by itself.', 'Why is the field outside an ideal infinite solenoid zero?')
c('Magnetostatics','Vector potential',['B = ∇×A','Coulomb gauge: ∇·A = 0','∇²A = −μ₀J (magnetostatics)','A = (μ₀/4π) ∫ J(r′)/R dτ′'], 'The vector potential automatically enforces zero magnetic divergence. Gauge freedom means different A fields may describe the same B.', 'In unbounded vacuum with localized steady current, use the integral solution, then curl A. Account for boundary conditions in bounded domains.', 'A is not unique. The stated Poisson equation assumes the Coulomb gauge and steady sources.', 'How does A change without changing B?')
c('Magnetostatics','Magnetic dipole & force',['m = I a n̂ (planar loop)','τ = m×B; U = −m·B','F = ∇(m·B) (fixed m)','B dip = μ₀[3(m·r̂)r̂−m]/(4πr³)'], 'A small current loop behaves as a magnetic dipole. A uniform field can produce torque; a nonuniform field can also produce net force.', 'Use the right-hand rule to orient m. Apply the dipole approximation only far from the source and where external B varies slowly across the loop.', 'The dipole field shown is the exterior field, not the distributional field at the origin.', 'Can a uniform magnetic field exert a net force on a localized closed current loop?')
c('Induction','Faraday & Lenz',['ℰ = ∮(E+vwire×B)·dl','ℰ = −dΦB/dt','ΦB = ∫ B·da'], 'Emf is work per unit charge around a circuit. It can arise from a time-varying field or motion through a field. Lenz’s sign opposes the change in linked flux.', 'Choose a loop orientation and its right-hand normal. Differentiate the linked flux including geometry changes. For ordinary smoothly moving circuits the flux rule gives total emf.', 'Induced current opposes the flux change, not necessarily the magnetic field itself. Sliding-contact geometries require careful circuit definition.', 'A loop enters a field region. Which way does its induced current flow?')
c('Induction','Inductance',['Flux linkage = LI','ℰself = −L dI/dt','U = ½LI²','ℰ₂ = −M dI₁/dt'], 'Inductance measures flux linkage per current in a linear fixed geometry. Mutual inductance measures coupling between circuits.', 'Compute B, flux per turn and total linked flux. In a passive pair, |M| ≤ √(L₁L₂); signs depend on winding orientations.', 'For N turns, flux linkage is NΦ, not Φ. Nonlinear materials can make L current dependent.', 'Derive L for a long solenoid and its stored energy.')
c('Energy','Poynting theorem',['u = ½ε₀E² + B²/(2μ₀)','S = E×B/μ₀','∂u/∂t + ∇·S = −J·E'], 'Field energy decreases when it flows out or does work on charges. S gives energy flux; J·E gives power transferred to matter per volume.', 'Dot Ampère–Maxwell with E and Faraday with B/μ₀. Subtract and use ∇·(E×B) = B·(∇×E) − E·(∇×B).', 'In material media, ½(E·D+B·H) requires linear, nondispersive, lossless response for the usual stored-energy interpretation.', 'Where does the energy entering a resistor flow?')
c('Energy','Momentum & stress',['g = S/c² = ε₀ E×B','Tᵢⱼ = ε₀(EᵢEⱼ−½δᵢⱼE²) + (BᵢBⱼ−½δᵢⱼB²)/μ₀','Fmatter = ∮ T·n̂ da − d/dt ∫ g dτ'], 'Electromagnetic fields carry momentum. The Maxwell stress tensor accounts for field stresses across a closed surface.', 'Use vacuum fields and an outward surface normal. Include the time derivative of field momentum when the fields are not steady.', 'The vacuum momentum expression should not be applied blindly to macroscopic media; material momentum also matters.', 'Why can light exert pressure although it has no rest mass?')
c('Waves','Vacuum wave equation',['∇²E − (1/c²)∂²E/∂t² = 0','∇²B − (1/c²)∂²B/∂t² = 0'], 'In source-free vacuum, changing electric and magnetic fields support propagating electromagnetic waves.', 'Curl Faraday; substitute Ampère–Maxwell; use the curl-curl identity and ∇·E = 0. Repeat for B.', 'This homogeneous form assumes ρ = 0 and J = 0. With sources the equation has driving terms.', 'Derive the electric wave equation step by step.')
c('Waves','Plane waves',['E = E₀ cos(k·r−ωt+φ)','ω = ck; k·E₀ = k·B₀ = 0','B = k̂×E/c','⟨S⟩ = ½ε₀cE₀² k̂'], 'A vacuum plane wave is transverse. E, B and propagation direction form a right-handed triad. E and B are in phase.', 'Apply divergence equations to prove transversality, then Faraday to fix the magnetic amplitude and direction.', 'E₀ here is peak amplitude; with RMS amplitude the factor ½ is absent. General fields need not satisfy E = cB.', 'What happens to B when the propagation direction reverses?')
c('Waves','Polarization',['Eₓ = A cosψ','Eᵧ = B cos(ψ+δ)','ψ = kz−ωt'], 'The tip of the electric vector traces a line, ellipse or circle at a fixed point. Polarization describes this transverse motion.', 'δ = 0 or π gives linear polarization. Equal nonzero amplitudes and δ = ±π/2 give circular polarization. Other generic combinations give elliptical polarization.', 'Right/left circular labels depend on the viewing and phase convention; state the convention before assigning handedness.', 'Which amplitudes and phases make a circularly polarized wave?')
c('Waves','Reflection at normal incidence',['Z = √(μ/ε)','rE = (Z₂−Z₁)/(Z₂+Z₁)','tE = 2Z₂/(Z₁+Z₂)','R = |rE|²; T = (Z₁/Z₂)|tE|²'], 'For lossless positive-index media at normal incidence, impedance mismatch reflects part of a wave. rE and tE refer to electric-field amplitudes.', 'Enforce tangential E and H continuity with no free surface current. Reverse the H direction for the reflected wave. Check R+T = 1.', 'T is not generally |tE|² because the transmitted impedance differs. These power formulas assume real positive impedances.', 'Why can tE exceed one without violating energy conservation?')
c('Waves','Oblique incidence',['n₁ sinθᵢ = n₂ sinθₜ','θreflected = θᵢ','tanθB = n₂/n₁ (μ₁ = μ₂)','sinθc = n₂/n₁ (n₁ > n₂)'], 'Phase matching along the interface gives Snell’s law. Fresnel amplitudes depend on polarization. Brewster incidence suppresses p-polarized reflection for ordinary lossless nonmagnetic dielectrics.', 'Angles are measured from the normal. Resolve fields into s (E perpendicular to incidence plane) and p (E in that plane), then match tangential fields.', 'The critical angle applies from higher to lower index. Under total internal reflection the second medium has an evanescent field, not zero field.', 'What is the difference between Brewster angle and critical angle?')
c('Waves','Conductors & skin depth',['J = σE','∇²E = με ∂²E/∂t² + μσ ∂E/∂t','δ = √[2/(μσω)]'], 'In a good conductor, oscillating fields decay over the skin depth δ. Energy is dissipated through Joule heating.', 'For e^(−iωt), use k² = μεω² + iμσω. In the good-conductor limit σ ≫ ωε, k ≈ (1+i)/δ for a wave proportional to e^(ikz).', 'The skin-depth approximation fails outside the good-conductor regime. Here σ means conductivity, not surface charge.', 'Why does high-frequency current concentrate near a conductor’s surface?')
c('Waves','Waveguides & cutoff',['β² = ω²με − kc²','TE: Ez = 0; TM: Hz = 0','Rectangular kc² = (mπ/a)²+(nπ/b)²','ωc = kc/√(με)'], 'Conducting walls enforce boundary conditions that permit discrete transverse modes. Propagation along the guide requires real β.', 'For a hollow rectangular perfect-conductor guide, TE has m,n ≥ 0 but not both zero; TM requires m,n ≥ 1. The dominant mode for a > b is TE₁₀.', 'A simply connected hollow guide has no TEM mode. Below cutoff the mode is evanescent.', 'Why does a hollow waveguide have a minimum propagation frequency?')
c('Potentials','Gauge transformations',['B = ∇×A','E = −∇V − ∂A/∂t','A′ = A + ∇χ','V′ = V − ∂χ/∂t'], 'Physical electric and magnetic fields are unchanged by gauge transformations. Potentials are useful but not uniquely determined.', 'Substitute the transformed potentials into E and B. The mixed derivatives cancel in E and the curl of a gradient vanishes in B.', 'E = −∇V alone applies only when the induction term can be omitted.', 'Verify explicitly that a gauge transformation preserves E.')
c('Potentials','Lorenz gauge & retardation',['∇·A + (1/c²)∂V/∂t = 0','(∇²−c⁻²∂t²)V = −ρ/ε₀','(∇²−c⁻²∂t²)A = −μ₀J','V = (1/4πε₀) ∫ ρ(r′,t−R/c)/R dτ′'], 'In the Lorenz gauge, potentials obey driven wave equations. Retarded solutions encode finite-speed propagation from sources.', 'The companion expression is A = (μ₀/4π)∫J(r′,t−R/c)/R dτ′. These select outgoing/causal solutions without additional incoming homogeneous fields.', 'Lorenz gauge is named after Ludvig Lorenz. Retarded time depends on source-observer separation inside the integral.', 'Why are instantaneous Coulomb potentials insufficient for general time-dependent sources?')
c('Radiation','Electric dipole radiation',['Erad = [n̂×(n̂×p̈)]/(4πε₀c²r)','Brad = n̂×Erad/c','P = |p̈|²/(6πε₀c³)'], 'An accelerating dipole produces transverse far-zone fields proportional to 1/r and radiated flux proportional to 1/r². Evaluate p̈ at retarded time t−r/c.', 'Use a localized, nonrelativistic electric-dipole source with size much less than a wavelength and observation distance in the radiation zone. Integrate the sin²θ pattern over solid angle.', 'The total-power formula is instantaneous in retarded source time. For p = p₀cosωt, average P = p₀²ω⁴/(12πε₀c³).', 'Why does dipole radiation vanish along the dipole axis?')
c('Radiation','Larmor & radiation pressure',['P = q²a²/(6πε₀c³)','Absorber: pressure = I/c','Perfect reflector: pressure = 2I/c'], 'A nonrelativistic accelerating point charge radiates. Light transfers momentum to surfaces; reflection can transfer twice the incident normal momentum.', 'Use the Larmor expression only for v ≪ c. The pressure formulas assume normal incidence in vacuum and ideal complete absorption or reflection.', 'Constant velocity alone does not cause vacuum radiation from an isolated charge. The relativistic radiation formula is different.', 'Why does reflection double radiation pressure at normal incidence?')
c('Relativity','Field transformations',['E′∥ = E∥; B′∥ = B∥','E′⊥ = γ(E⊥ + v×B)','B′⊥ = γ(B⊥ − v×E/c²)','γ = 1/√(1−v²/c²)'], 'Electric and magnetic fields mix under Lorentz boosts. These formulas describe frame S′ moving with velocity v relative to S.', 'Split each field parallel and perpendicular to v, apply the transformation, then recombine. Sources and forces must be transformed consistently.', 'Do not confuse a change of observer with a particle’s velocity inside one fixed frame.', 'Can a purely electric field in one frame have a magnetic component in another?')
c('Relativity','Lorentz invariants',['E·B is invariant','E²−c²B² is invariant'], 'These combinations have the same value in every inertial frame. They constrain whether a field can be transformed to a purely electric or purely magnetic one.', 'For nonzero fields with E·B = 0: positive E²−c²B² allows a pure-electric frame; negative allows a pure-magnetic frame. A nonzero null field has neither.', 'A vacuum plane wave has both invariants zero, but its fields cannot be transformed away.', 'Why can no inertial observer see a plane electromagnetic wave at rest?')
c('Worked examples','Uniform charged sphere',['r < a: E = ρr/(3ε₀) r̂','r ≥ a: E = Q/(4πε₀r²) r̂','Q = 4πρa³/3'], 'A solid nonconducting sphere of radius a has uniform volume charge density ρ.', '1. Spherical symmetry gives radial E. 2. Inside, Qenc = 4πρr³/3. 3. E(4πr²) = Qenc/ε₀. 4. Outside, enclose all Q. 5. Verify E is continuous at a and zero at the center.', 'For a conducting sphere in electrostatic equilibrium, interior E is zero instead; charge resides on the surface.', 'Derive the potential inside, taking V(∞) = 0. Answer: Q(3−r²/a²)/(8πε₀a).')
c('Worked examples','Charging capacitor',['E ≈ Q/(ε₀πa²)','r < a: B = μ₀Ir/(2πa²)','r > a: B = μ₀I/(2πr)'], 'A circular parallel-plate capacitor of radius a is charged with current I. Neglect fringing and use the quasistatic approximation.', '1. Choose a coaxial circle in the gap. 2. Enclosed electric flux for r < a is Eπr². 3. Use I = dQ/dt. 4. Ampère–Maxwell gives B2πr = μ₀I r²/a². 5. Outside use the full plate flux.', 'There is no conduction current across an ideal vacuum gap; displacement current accounts for the magnetic circulation.', 'Show that the displacement current through the whole gap equals I.')
c('Worked examples','Boundary: two dielectrics',['ε₁E₁n = ε₂E₂n','E₁t = E₂t','tanθ₂/tanθ₁ = ε₂/ε₁'], 'Two linear isotropic dielectrics meet with no free surface charge. θ is the angle between E and the normal, not a ray propagation angle.', '1. Split E into normal and tangential components. 2. Apply normal D continuity. 3. Apply tangential E continuity. 4. Divide Et by En to obtain the angle relation.', 'This is a relation for electrostatic field lines. It is not Snell’s law for propagating waves.', 'If ε₂ > ε₁, do the field lines in medium 2 lean toward or away from the normal?')
c('Exam method','How to structure a solution',['Geometry → assumptions → laws','Symmetry → equations → boundaries','Solve → units → limiting cases'], 'A strong exam solution explains why the selected law and approximations apply, then carries out the mathematics.', 'Name the regime (static, quasistatic, wave, radiation), coordinate system, material model and sources. Write the governing equations, justify symmetry, impose boundaries and test the result.', 'A memorized formula without its geometry, regime and boundary conditions can be incorrect even if the algebra is flawless.', 'Can you explain the physical meaning of every term in your final equation?')
Path('public/content.js').write_text('const CARDS = '+json.dumps(cards,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+';\n')
print(len(cards),'cards')
